WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.287
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.287 --> 00:00:10.181
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Let's do a thought experiment.

00:00:10.181 --> 00:00:12.958
Everyone close your eyes.

00:00:12.958 --> 00:00:16.847
Let's say when you walk out of here,
you see a shiny object in the bushes.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:19.420
You bend down and
you find it's a magic lamp.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.040
You rub it and a genie comes out.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.750
And the genie says, hey,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.410
I'm actually a genie in training so
I can only grant you one wish.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.330
But here it is, if you tell me
the one thing you want to do in life

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.130
with no fear of failure,
I'll make sure you're successful.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:40.910
It may not be a week from now,
a year from now, or a decade from now.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.490
But once you tell me, success will follow.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:45.500
Open your eyes.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.670
What would you ask the genie for?

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.500
A year and a half ago, my fourth
week at Stamford Business School,

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.280
an instructor named Graham Weaver
challenged my class with this assignment.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:59.430
Later that night,
I went home to think about it.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.300
But after an hour, I was stumped.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.830
I had no idea what to ask the genie for.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:10.040
You see, I, like many of us, had grown
up learning how to think practically and

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.230
that meant going after the highest paying,
most prestigious jobs available.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.250
The formula was, put in your dues first,
fulfillment second.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.650
And until I came into business school,
I lived that.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.970
In college, I studied to make As, and then
when I graduated I took a corporate job.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.240
And put my head down.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.220
Never stopping to think about
what I actually wanted.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.130
You see, I was confusing busyness and
obligation with a sense of purpose.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.402
So I was sitting there thinking about
the genie, I realized I needed to rewind,

00:01:38.402 --> 00:01:42.400
to go back through all the things
that had excited me in the past.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.210
It wasn't that corporate job, nor

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.780
was it any of my hobbies that I had
in college, cooking or architecture.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.973
No, I had to go back even further,
all the way to high school.

00:01:52.973 --> 00:01:56.050
As a kid, I loved stories.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.810
I was pretty awkward.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.510
So in lieu of real friends, I had the
characters from Mice and Men, Catch 22,

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.060
The Old Man and the Sea.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.640
Stories were pure magic,
it transported me to a different world,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.670
the time I felt most alive was when I was
reading, falling into living stories.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.400
So I decided to act.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.910
The next quarter,
that first week of January,

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.490
over a year ago, I walked into
an undergraduate creative writing course.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.310
Now to be honest, I don't remember
the material we covered on that first day.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.800
What I do remember is that for two hours
I didn't even think about my phone.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.910
I was on a different planet,
not a different department.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:39.530
When I left class, I was tingly, something
I haven't felt since high school.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.740
For the last year,
I followed this feeling.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.379
I've taken four more fiction courses.

00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:50.853
And this winter, I submitted applications
for MFA programs in creative writing.

00:02:50.853 --> 00:02:53.046
And I love it.

00:02:53.046 --> 00:02:55.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I wake up at 5, 4, 3 in the morning,

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.760
just to squeeze in a few
extra hours of writing.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.110
I read a short story everyday.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.734
And I aim for a novel week.

00:03:01.734 --> 00:03:03.750
I can't get enough.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.580
But leaving in a secure world of business
for the unsure one of fiction writing.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.510
Following my MBA with an MFA is risky.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.350
And that is far away from
practical as you can get.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.890
For me and for many of us, there's a
tremendous tension between the things that

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.500
put food on the table and
those that excite us.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.790
This tension was studied
by famed psychologist,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.280
Abraham Maslow,
who combined anecdotal evidence

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.080
with stories from successful individuals
to create the Hierarchy Of Needs.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.360
Our primary needs,
Maslow said, are physiological.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.845
That's food, water, sex.

00:03:37.845 --> 00:03:43.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Once

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:46.920
those are taken care of we seek safety,
that's shelter.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.880
And only once if you're protected do we go
after first love, then self-esteem, and

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.530
finally at the very peak of
the pyramid self-actualization or

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.090
what we might call fulfillment.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.080
Growing up, I subscribed to this model.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.680
Over the past year, though,
I started thinking about it differently.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.380
What if we change the formula?

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.960
What if, instead of fulfillment last,
we put fulfillment first, and

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.060
trust that the rest will follow?

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:18.620
Now, I fully understand that to even have
a conversation about self-actualization

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.980
is a luxury and a privilege.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.500
But when we're more engaged in our careers
and in our lives, we're more effective.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.870
We're better able to put food on
the table and roofs over our heads.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.220
Leading with fulfillment isn't
ignoring those primary needs.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.330
It's focusing on what's
really important so

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:37.330
that we can find fulfillment
while we meet those needs.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:39.740
And actually our brains are wired for
this.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.300
You see, on the outside,
we have the neocortex.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.030
It's the part responsible for
rational thought, and

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:47.410
it helps us put our heads down and
work at any given task.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:51.240
But beneath that is the limbic brain,
related to emotion and fulfillment.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.330
And it's this part that actually
drives and motivates our behavior.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:56.640
So how do we do this?

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.210
How do we start with fulfillment?

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.680
In my experience, there are two steps.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.425
First, find that thing that lights you up,
second go after it.

00:05:05.425 --> 00:05:08.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right, so that sounds easy, but

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:09.850
obviously it's not.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:11.310
So let's unpack it.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.160
First, find the thing that lights you up.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.360
This is the genie exercise,
what would you ask the genie for?

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.301
Sometime this week,
take just ten minutes to think about it.

00:05:22.301 --> 00:05:25.830
Maybe it's that childhood passion
you've been ignoring for years or

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:29.957
perhaps it's that thing you find yourself
doing when you're procrastinating in

00:05:29.957 --> 00:05:31.230
your studies or at work.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.070
And step two, go after this thing.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:36.550
Learn taekwondo,
start a fashion website for dogs,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.835
or dedicate your life to this kind of
cash flow analysis, if that's your thing.

00:05:39.835 --> 00:05:42.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Whatever it is, go after it.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.190
And every week, continue to take those
ten minutes to check in with yourself.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.010
Ask, is this thing still firing me up?

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.510
If it is, awesome.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.660
Keep at it.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.340
If it's not, and
you have to be honest with yourself here,

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:58.410
go back to step one, back to the genie.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.600
And this, this is the hardest part.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:01.980
This is the part nobody talks about.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.293
If you're like me,
you won't find the thing on the first try.

00:06:05.293 --> 00:06:08.527
It will be the 4th, or
the 5th, or the 15th.

00:06:08.527 --> 00:06:11.253
When cooking was my thing,
I started filling out applications for

00:06:11.253 --> 00:06:12.780
culinary school.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:16.670
When it was architecture, I picked up a
civil engineering degree as an undergrad.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.510
None of those pursuits are my thing,
they didn't fire me up.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.390
So I gave up on culinary school and

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.020
let my engineering degree
gather dust on the shelf.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.950
This is the part nobody talks about
because the act of trying, and failing,

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:35.400
and moving on, trying, and failing,
and moving on, is mucky, and

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.700
disheartening, and it's embarrassing.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:39.280
When you try something and
it doesn't work out,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.250
it's almost impossible to go back to
the genie and ask for yet another thing.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.100
But we have to.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:49.750
The punch line of the genie
exercise is that you are the genie.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:51.590
You have the ability to
grant yourself this wish.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:55.262
You have to trust that no
matter how many things you try,

00:06:55.262 --> 00:06:59.246
no matter how low you get,
that when you go after this thing,

00:06:59.246 --> 00:07:03.808
when you find your creative writing,
everything else will follow.

00:07:05.608 --> 00:07:08.653
Decades ago, Abraham Maslow
described self actualization,

00:07:08.653 --> 00:07:12.215
something achieved by the likes of
Thomas Jefferson, Abraham Lincoln,

00:07:12.215 --> 00:07:14.830
Albert Einstein, as becoming fully human.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.760
And one of the key characteristics of
these individuals was their ability to

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:23.470
have peak experiences, feelings of
ecstasy, harmony and deep meaning,

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:28.770
feeling at one with the universe,
stronger and calmer than ever.

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:33.420
Now I'm no Lincoln or Einstein but when
I write, I get a taste of this feeling.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.070
And I'm up here today because
I want you to feel it too.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.870
To become human, to be self-actualized,

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.170
to earn those feelings of ecstasy and
deep meaning.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:45.940
And you don't have to win the Nobel prize
or totally change your career to do it.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.260
Fulfillment is a life-long
investment banker,

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.729
starting a dairy farm in upstate New York.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.806
It's a single mom who volunteers in
the soup kitchen every Thursday night.

00:07:54.806 --> 00:07:56.472
It's part-time,

00:07:56.472 --> 00:08:01.150
it's full-time, it's a week from now,
a year from now, a decade from now.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:04.180
It's out there for you and for me.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.710
Now, I don't have it all figured out.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:08.820
If I can't hack it as a writer,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.015
I'm going to have to get another
job that will pay the bills.

00:08:11.015 --> 00:08:13.423
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But for now,

00:08:13.423 --> 00:08:19.404
I'm putting fulfillment first and
trusting that the rest will follow.

00:08:19.404 --> 00:08:20.767
Almost.

00:08:20.767 --> 00:08:24.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What will you accomplish

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.970
when you find the thing
that lights you up?

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.549
What will you ask the genie for?

00:08:30.549 --> 00:08:32.272
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:32.272 --> 00:08:36.658
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

